NNAAMMEE
     ddddbb — interactive kernel debugger

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     In order to enable kernel debugging facilities include:

           ooppttiioonnss KKDDBB
           ooppttiioonnss DDDDBB

     To prevent activation of the debugger on kernel panic(9):

           ooppttiioonnss KKDDBB__UUNNAATTTTEENNDDEEDD

     In order to print a stack trace of the current thread on the console for
     a panic:

           ooppttiioonnss KKDDBB__TTRRAACCEE

     To print the numerical value of symbols in addition to the symbolic rep‐
     resentation, define:

           ooppttiioonnss DDDDBB__NNUUMMSSYYMM

     To enable the gdb(4) backend, so that remote debugging with kgdb(1)
     (_p_o_r_t_s_/_d_e_v_e_l_/_g_d_b) is possible, include:

           ooppttiioonnss GGDDBB

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddddbb kernel debugger is an interactive debugger with a syntax inspired
     by gdb(1) (_p_o_r_t_s_/_d_e_v_e_l_/_g_d_b).  If linked into the running kernel, it can
     be invoked locally with the ‘debug’ keymap(5) action, usually mapped to
     Ctrl+Alt+Esc, or by setting the _d_e_b_u_g_._k_d_b_._e_n_t_e_r sysctl to 1.  The debug‐
     ger is also invoked on kernel panic(9) if the _d_e_b_u_g_._d_e_b_u_g_g_e_r___o_n___p_a_n_i_c
     sysctl(8) MIB variable is set non-zero, which is the default unless the
     KDB_UNATTENDED option is specified.  Similarly, if the
     _d_e_b_u_g_._d_e_b_u_g_g_e_r___o_n___r_e_c_u_r_s_i_v_e___p_a_n_i_c variable is set to 1, then the debugger
     will be invoked on a recursive kernel panic.  This variable has a default
     value of 0, and has no effect if _d_e_b_u_g_._d_e_b_u_g_g_e_r___o_n___p_a_n_i_c is already set
     non-zero.

     The current location is called _d_o_t.  The _d_o_t is displayed with a hexadec‐
     imal format at a prompt.  The commands eexxaammiinnee and wwrriittee update _d_o_t to
     the address of the last line examined or the last location modified, and
     set _n_e_x_t to the address of the next location to be examined or changed.
     Other commands do not change _d_o_t, and set _n_e_x_t to be the same as _d_o_t.

     The general command syntax is: _c_o_m_m_a_n_d[/_m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r] [_a_d_d_r][,_c_o_u_n_t]

     A blank line repeats the previous command from the address _n_e_x_t with
     count 1 and no modifiers.  Specifying _a_d_d_r sets _d_o_t to the address.
     Omitting _a_d_d_r uses _d_o_t.  A missing _c_o_u_n_t is taken to be 1 for printing
     commands or infinity for stack traces.  A _c_o_u_n_t of -1 is equivalent to a
     missing _c_o_u_n_t.  Options that are supplied but not supported by the given
     _c_o_m_m_a_n_d are usually ignored.

     The ddddbb debugger has a pager feature (like the more(1) command) for the
     output.  If an output line exceeds the number set in the _l_i_n_e_s variable,
     it displays “--More--” and waits for a response.  The valid responses for
     it are:

     SPC  one more page
     RET  one more line
     q    abort the current command, and return to the command input mode

     Finally, ddddbb provides a small (currently 10 items) command history, and
     offers simple eemmaaccss-style command line editing capabilities.  In addition
     to the eemmaaccss control keys, the usual ANSI arrow keys may be used to
     browse through the history buffer, and move the cursor within the current
     line.

