     The debugger accesses registers and variables as $_n_a_m_e.  Register names
     are as in the “sshhooww rreeggiisstteerrss” command.  Some variables are suffixed with
     numbers, and may have some modifier following a colon immediately after
     the variable name.  For example, register variables can have a uu modifier
     to indicate user register (e.g., “$eax:u”).

     Built-in variables currently supported are:

     _r_a_d_i_x     Input and output radix.
     _m_a_x_o_f_f    Addresses are printed as “_s_y_m_b_o_l+_o_f_f_s_e_t” unless _o_f_f_s_e_t is
               greater than _m_a_x_o_f_f.
     _m_a_x_w_i_d_t_h  The width of the displayed line.
     _l_i_n_e_s     The number of lines.  It is used by the built-in pager.  Set‐
               ting it to 0 disables paging.
     _t_a_b_s_t_o_p_s  Tab stop width.
     _w_o_r_k_x_x    Work variable; _x_x can take values from 0 to 31.

EEXXPPRREESSSSIIOONNSS
     Most expression operators in C are supported except ‘~’, ‘^’, and unary
     ‘&’.  Special rules in ddddbb are:

     Identifiers  The name of a symbol is translated to the value of the sym‐
                  bol, which is the address of the corresponding object.  ‘.’
                  and ‘:’ can be used in the identifier.  If supported by an
                  object format dependent routine, [_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e:]_f_u_n_c:_l_i_n_e_n_o,
                  [_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e:]_v_a_r_i_a_b_l_e, and [_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e:]_l_i_n_e_n_o can be accepted
                  as a symbol.

     Numbers      Radix is determined by the first two letters: ‘0x’: hex,
                  ‘0o’: octal, ‘0t’: decimal; otherwise, follow current radix.

     .            _d_o_t

     +            _n_e_x_t

     ..           address of the start of the last line examined.  Unlike _d_o_t
                  or _n_e_x_t, this is only changed by eexxaammiinnee or wwrriittee command.

     '            last address explicitly specified.

     $_v_a_r_i_a_b_l_e    Translated to the value of the specified variable.  It may
                  be followed by a ‘:’ and modifiers as described above.

     _a#_b          A binary operator which rounds up the left hand side to the
                  next multiple of right hand side.

     *_e_x_p_r        Indirection.  It may be followed by a ‘:’ and modifiers as
                  described above.

SSCCRRIIPPTTIINNGG
     ddddbb supports a basic scripting facility to allow automating tasks or re‐
     sponses to specific events.  Each script consists of a list of DDB com‐
     mands to be executed sequentially, and is assigned a unique name.  Cer‐
     tain script names have special meaning, and will be automatically run on
     various ddddbb events if scripts by those names have been defined.

     The ssccrriipptt command may be used to define a script by name.  Scripts con‐
     sist of a series of ddddbb commands separated with the ‘;’ character.  For
     example:

           script kdb.enter.panic=bt; show pcpu
           script lockinfo=show alllocks; show lockedvnods

     The ssccrriippttss command lists currently defined scripts.

     The rruunn command execute a script by name.  For example:

           run lockinfo

     The uunnssccrriipptt command may be used to delete a script by name.  For exam‐
     ple:

           unscript kdb.enter.panic

     These functions may also be performed from userspace using the ddb(8)
     command.

